+## Bottom line
+
+For this 38-year-old with sneezing, an itchy nose, and itchy, watery eyes, loratadine is the more practical starting choice when staying alert for weekday driving is the priority. Both medicines treat these seasonal allergy symptoms and both can cause drowsiness, but the cetirizine information specifically warns not to drive until its effects are known. Loratadine can still cause drowsiness or tiredness, so this choice does not guarantee alertness. Individual response varies, and the government sources reviewed do not establish that one is universally more effective for every person.
+
+Take the first dose when he will not need to drive, then notice both symptom relief and alertness before driving on a later day. Choose a single-ingredient product, read its Drug Facts label, and use it exactly as directed. If it causes sleepiness, slowed thinking, or poor focus, he should not drive.
+
+## Side-by-side
+
+| Medicine | What the sources say it treats | Label-use basics | Alertness and other common effects | Fit for this situation |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Cetirizine | Sneezing; runny or itchy nose or throat; red, itchy, or watery eyes | Taken once daily, with or without food; follow the package directions and do not take more often than directed | May cause drowsiness, excessive tiredness, and dry mouth; its patient information says not to drive until its effect is known | A reasonable allergy option, but the explicit drowsiness and driving warning makes it the less convenient first trial for a frequent daytime driver |
+| Loratadine | Sneezing; runny nose; itching of the nose or throat; red, itchy, or watery eyes | Taken once daily, with or without food; follow the package directions and do not take more or take it more often than directed | Drowsiness, tiredness, and headache are listed possible effects | The better starting fit for the stated driving priority, while still requiring a cautious first trial because drowsiness remains possible |
+
+This is not a head-to-head effectiveness verdict. The two medicine pages describe uses and risks separately; they do not prove which one will control his particular symptoms better. If the first choice does not help enough or affects alertness, a pharmacist or clinician can help reassess rather than simply adding the other medicine.
+
+## Safety for this situation
+
+He takes lisinopril 10 mg daily for controlled high blood pressure and reports no kidney disease, no liver disease, and no asthma. The medicine pages advise telling a health professional about all prescription and nonprescription products and about kidney or liver disease. Those conditions are absent here, but the cited consumer pages do not provide a personalized interaction assessment for lisinopril. A pharmacist can check the exact single-ingredient product against his current medication list if he wants an individualized interaction review.
+
+Use only one of these two antihistamines at a time; taking them together falls outside this comparison. Follow the Drug Facts or package label rather than improvising a dose, and do not double a missed dose. Avoid alcohol during the first trial because it can worsen drowsiness. Store the medicine out of children’s reach.
+
+Driving deserves a separate check even if he does not feel obviously sleepy: antihistamines may slow reaction time or make it harder to focus. The FDA advises taking a new nonprescription medicine for the first time when driving will not be necessary. If he feels drowsy, dizzy, confused, unfocused, or slowed, he should not drive or operate machinery.
+
+## When to get help
+
+Call 911 for trouble breathing or swallowing, swelling of the face, lips, mouth, tongue, or throat, collapse, a seizure, or inability to wake. Stop the suspected medicine while emergency help is being obtained; an oral antihistamine is not a substitute for emergency care.
+
+Contact a clinician promptly for wheezing, significant swelling, a severe rash, or unusual symptoms. Although he currently has no asthma, new wheezing or breathing symptoms need assessment. Arrange routine advice if his sneezing, itchy nose, or itchy, watery eyes remain troublesome despite label-directed use, if side effects interfere with driving or daily life, or if he is unsure whether the symptoms are allergies. A pharmacist is also an appropriate first stop for the medication-list and label check.
